        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "AI findings\r\n\r\n\r\n>  1. **`:tabonly` can close the wrong tab.** `ex_tabonly`'s guard `tp->tp_topframe != topframe`\r\n>     re-reads the live `topframe` each pass, so an autocmd switching to a *third* tab makes it destroy\r\n>     the tab that was current. Repro: 3 tabs, `:tabonly` from tab 3, `autocmd WinClosed * ++once`\r\n>     calling `nvim_set_current_tabpage(tab2)` — only tab 2 survives. Capturing the keep-tabpage once\r\n>     in `ex_tabonly` fixes it and subsumes the `tp == curtab` check added here.\r\n>  \r\n>  2. **Infinite loop in `tabpage_close()`**: `while (curwin->w_floating) { ex_win_close(forceit,\r\n>     curwin, NULL); }`. `ex_win_close` returns void, so an early bail (`no_write_message()`,\r\n>     `is_ctx_win()`, `window_layout_locked()`) leaves `curwin` unchanged and the loop never\r\n>     terminates. Repro: `set nohidden`, `:tabnew`, float via `nvim_open_win` on a modified buffer with\r\n>     `b_nwindows == 1`, then `:tabclose`. Pre-existing, and missing the 1000-iteration bound the\r\n>     sibling loop has.\r\n>  \r\n>  3. **`tp->tp_lastwin == wp` compares a `wp` autocmds may have freed** — in this PR's own repro,\r\n>     `nvim_buf_delete` inside WinClosed frees it via `close_windows()`. UB, plus a spurious \"close\r\n>     failed\" if the allocator reuses the address. `win_valid_any_tab(wp)`, or comparing handles rather\r\n>     than pointers, would be sound.\r\n>  \r\n>  4. **The new `tp == curtab` guard runs after `ex_win_close`**, but `trigger_tabclosedpre(tp)` runs\r\n>     before it and can leave `curtab == tp` on its own: it restores `ptp` only `if\r\n>     (valid_tabpage(ptp))`, else falls back to `first_tabpage`. If that is `tp`, the first iteration\r\n>     trips the new assert. Moving the check to the top of the loop body covers both.\r\n>  \r\n>  5. **The abort is silent** — `:tabclose N` / `:tabonly` become a no-op with no message and no\r\n>     `v:errmsg`, so scripts can't distinguish it from success.\r\n>  \r\n>  6. **The new test asserts only `assert_alive()`**, so it won't catch a regression that closes the\r\n>     wrong tab. `tabpagenr('$') == 2`, `tabpagenr() == 1`, `#nvim_list_wins() == 2` would pin it. The\r\n>     name also mentions `:tabclose`, but only `:tabonly` is exercised — `:tabclose N` is a separate\r\n>     entry point into `tabpage_close_other`.\r\n>  \r\n>  7. The comment says the guard aborts \"a `:tabonly`\", but `tabpage_close_other` is also `:tabclose N`\r\n>     (ex_docmd.c:5230) and mouse tabline close (mouse.c:160).",
